.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 22, 2026
.Dt DEVD 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm devd
.Nd device event daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dn
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl t Ar timeout
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon reads the kernel's device events from
.Xr devctl 4
and runs the commands its configuration gives for them: mounting a CD's disc
when one is put in, logging a drive plugged in or pulled out.
It is started at boot by
.Pa /etc/rc.d/devd
when
.Va devd_enable
is
.Dq YES
in
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
The kernel keeps the events of boot until
.Nm
reads them, so its rules see the drives found at boot as if they had just been
plugged in.
.Pp
The configuration is a shell script, described in
.Xr devd.conf 5 :
.Pa /etc/devd.conf ,
then every
.Pa *.conf
file in
.Pa /etc/devd
and then in
.Pa /usr/local/etc/devd ,
in lexical order, all run by one shell built into
.Nm .
Its
.Ic on
command adds a rule.
A file that does not parse is reported and skipped; the others still load.
.Pp
For each event,
.Nm
first writes its line to every client of
.Pa /var/run/devd.pipe ,
then finds the rules it matches.
If any match, a child process sets the shell variables
.Va verb ,
.Va class
and one for each of the event's fields, exports them, and runs each matching
rule's handler in turn.
.Nm
waits for the child before it reads the next event, so handlers run in the
order the events happened; a handler that should not hold the next event up
runs its commands in the background
.Pq Ql & .
Nothing a handler changes, such as a variable or the directory, outlives its
child.
A handler that exits with a non-zero status is logged with its event.
.Pp
.Pa /var/run/devd.pipe
is a
.Xr unix 4
stream socket that anyone may connect to, for programs that follow the events
as they happen: a client reads lines from it as from
.Pa /dev/devctl .
A client gets each event line read after it connected, none from before.
One that falls behind, so that a line can't be written to it at once, is
disconnected.
What clients send is discarded.
.Pp
.Nm
logs to
.Xr syslogd 8
with the
.Li daemon
facility.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d
Stay in the foreground, and log each event and the rules it matched to the
standard error too.
.It Fl f Ar file
Read only
.Ar file
as the configuration.
.It Fl n
Check the configuration and exit: status 0 if every file loaded without a
problem, 1 otherwise, with each problem on the standard error.
.It Fl t Ar timeout
A handler's child still running after
.Ar timeout
seconds is killed, with its process group, and
.Nm
goes on to the next event.
The default is 30; 0 sets no limit.
.El
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width SIGTERM
.It Dv SIGHUP
Load the configuration again, into a new shell.
.It Dv SIGTERM , Dv SIGINT
Remove the socket and the pid file, and exit.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/local/etc/devd/*.conf -compact
.It Pa /dev/devctl
the kernel's events
.It Pa /etc/devd.conf
the configuration
.It Pa /etc/devd/*.conf
local rules
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/devd/*.conf
packages' rules
.It Pa /var/run/devd.pipe
the event socket
.It Pa /var/run/devd.pid
the running daemon's process id
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
runs until a signal ends it, then exits 0; it exits 1 if it can't start:
another
.Nm
is running
.Po
.Pa /dev/devctl
is open
.Pc ,
or the socket or pid file can't be made.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sh 1 ,
.Xr devctl 4 ,
.Xr devd.conf 5 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr syslogd 8
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
daemon first appeared in
.Fx 5.0 .
OxideBSD's takes a shell script as its configuration where FreeBSD's has a
language of its own.
